  [1mI recommend word boundary.[0m The extra cost is one [38;5;153mlastIndexOf(' ')[39m and a guard, and it's paid once; the mid-word cut is
  the thing people file bugs about. The usual argument against it — unpredictable output width — doesn't apply here,
  because you've already decided [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m is a hard ceiling, so word-boundary only ever returns [3mshorter[0m, never longer.

  [1mShort design[0m

  [1mAPI:[0m flat option matching the existing style — [38;5;153mformat(str, { truncate: 20 })[39m, where the value is the max length.
  Guarded with [38;5;153mtypeof options.truncate === 'number'[39m rather than a truthy check, so [38;5;153mtruncate: 0[39m behaves as a real value
  instead of being silently skipped.

  [1mPlacement:[0m the truncate block goes [1mlast[0m, after [38;5;153mprefix[39m/[38;5;153msuffix[39m. That's the only ordering where [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m actually bounds
  the returned string, which is what you chose. The tradeoff: a long [38;5;153mprefix[39m eats into the budget and can itself be
  truncated. Say the word if you'd rather [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m bound only the core string before decoration.

  [1mEdge cases:[0m no-op when [38;5;153mstr.length <= maxLength[39m; when [38;5;153mmaxLength <= 3[39m there's no room for content, so return
  [38;5;153m'...'.slice(0, maxLength)[39m; single-word-too-long falls back to a hard cut.

  [1mFiles:[0m [38;5;153mformat.js[39m (one [38;5;153mif[39m block plus a small module-local helper), [38;5;153mformat.test.js[39m (new [38;5;153mconsole.assert[39m lines in the
  existing flat-script pattern — short string untouched, exact-boundary length, mid-word cut, word-boundary cut,
  long-word fallback, tiny [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m, and one composed with [38;5;153muppercase[39m/[38;5;153mprefix[39m). No new dependencies; run with [38;5;153mnode [39m
  [38;5;153mformat.test.js[39m.
